  [x] 4. Acceptance criteria for the slice, tied back to the owning tickets' criteria.
        build ticket 91: `twin/demo_slice.py`'s `ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA` — a table mapping this decision ticket's own four ACs to the build tickets that realise them (71/73/75 for subject selection, 77 for the boundary mechanism, 91 for the rendered artefact itself), emitted in the artefact body and printed by `twin demo-slice`. tests/test_demo_slice.py::test_summary_composes_all_four_pieces asserts all four indices are present, each naming at least one build ticket.
==> scenario-engine: full  (7/7 of decision ticket 13)
  [x] 1. The scenario object defined, incl. its references and its relationship to forecasts.
        build ticket 06: twin/verbs.py run() — a scenario references {components, graph version, world models, time} and emits forecasts as a list, always, one per named world model.
  [x] 2. Concrete semantics for fast-forward, rewind and play, each distinguished.
        build ticket 37: twin/verbs.py run() is fast-forward — projection with no intervention, demonstrated in tests/test_four_verbs.py::test_projection_is_run_with_no_intervention. twin/primitives.py rewind() is the time half of rewind (build ticket 35) and twin/regimes.py the information-gate half (build ticket 36). twin/primitives.py Do is play (build ticket 22). Build ticket 37 composes the two primitives into all four operations — projection alone, do() alone (act-now), rewind+do() (the counterfactual), rewind+run() (the backtest, `twin backtest`) — with no backtest-specific code path: harness guard backtest_is_a_pure_composition asserts an explicit rewind+run computes the identical forecast run() alone computes at the same time, differing only in which command produced it.
  [x] 3. Where scenarios live + how they are versioned/diffed.
        build ticket 88: Git already supplied the storage/versioning half (build ticket 06); the gap was the renderer. twin/scenario_diff.py's diff() (build ticket 88) takes a scenario id and two git refs — branch-per-scenario (research 03) or two commits on one branch alike, since ModelRepo.open(path, ref=...) already opens either — and reports a field-level diff of the scenario itself (question, proposition, at, horizon, components, world_models) plus a map-diff of every component either side's overlay places (Graph.wardley(), no separate authoring step). Wired as `twin scenario-diff` (twin/cli.py cmd_scenario_diff). tests/test_scenario_diff.py exercises a real repository with a real `explore` branch: a scalar field change and a component's evolution-stage regression both reported, an unchanged field/component not reported, identical refs diffing to nothing, a scenario authored on only one side reported via `scenario_present` rather than refused (mid-flight branch-per-scenario authoring is a real state), and absence at both sides refused.
  [x] 4. How opportunity/gameplay moves are proposed, with the negativity counterweight addressed.
        build ticket 46: twin/gameplay_lens.py propose() checks a two-play catalogue (land-grab, exploit-commoditisation) against the current map's evolution positions, needs-edge dependency structure and ownership, with the reason and the checked preconditions attached per opportunity. gameplay_lens.sweep() runs it unconditionally across every org overlay of every named repository, no human naming a component — the structural pull against the record's push (decision ticket 13 Q3) — and reports opportunity-candidate volume beside each org's bound-signal volume in the same artefact so the counterweight is measurable.
  [x] 5. Admissibility rule for the scenario library.
        build ticket 69: twin/schema.py's closed COMMITTED_SCENARIO_CLASSES enum and the optional scenario.class field make "belongs to the standing library" mechanical: a scenario is admissible by carrying one of the nine committed classes (or by being unconditionally swept per build ticket 09 — decision ticket 13's other admissibility arm). fixtures.build_library_org populates all nine; harness guard standing_library_covers_committed_classes (twin/invariants/checks.py) fails CI the moment one is silently dropped, demonstrated in tests/test_scenario_library.py::test_dropping_a_committed_class_from_the_library_fails_the_invariant. The full "preconditions/triggers checkable against the map" reading of admissibility — the precondition-triggered arm — stays for build ticket 46 (gameplay-lens); this ticket closes only the committed-set arm.
  [x] 6. A selection/prioritisation rule for which scenarios run (the combinatorics answer).
        build ticket 88: The four tiers decision ticket 13 names — standing library, precondition-triggered, event-triggered, ad-hoc — are proven the complete, non-overlapping set by checking the source directly (build ticket 88), the same discipline harness guard `backtest_is_a_pure_composition` already uses for a structural CLI-command claim. tests/test_scenario_selection_tiers.py: exactly two primitives can produce a scenario execution or an opportunity — verbs.run() and gameplay_lens.propose()/.sweep() — and every call site of either across twin/*.py is one of schedule.py (tier 1, standing library, unconditional loop over every scenario in every overlay — no scenario named), gameplay_lens.py (tier 2, unconditional map scan — no component named), or cli.py's cmd_run/cmd_backtest (tiers 3 and 4, event-triggered and ad-hoc, sharing the identical call because decision ticket 13 distinguishes them only by why a human or automation invoked it, not by a different code path); reproduce.py's replay() also calls verbs.run() but originates nothing — it reads the scenario id out of an already-emitted artefact's own recorded command, never a fresh argument. A fifth caller of either primitive anywhere in twin/*.py would fail the test.
  [x] 7. Exercised on one fear scenario and one opportunity scenario across the co-flagships.
        build ticket 88: Netflix already had both (build ticket 74): the fear scenario rewound under `as-consumed` (`twin backtest`) and the opportunity a `land-grab` on `streaming-service` pulled by `twin gameplay-sweep`. Intel had only its fear scenario on the real spine (`does-the-14a-bet-land-a-named-customer`, build ticket 75); its opportunity side existed only as the toy `euv-slip-2026` fixture citing `example.invalid`, unrelated to the real spine. Build ticket 88 adds `euv-readiness-wins-the-14a-opportunity` to `fixtures.build_intel_org` — the real, cited EUV causal edge build ticket 81 added (`euv-lithography` -> `leading-edge-foundry-node`), read the upside way: does the subject's own High-NA EUV tooling readiness hold through its own named 2026 go/no-go, positioning it to win the external customer commitment, the reverse of the historical slip the same edge's own note cites. Named honestly rather than forced into the identical shape Netflix's own opportunity took: `gameplay_lens.propose()` finds nothing to pull on this org's overlay (no person edges, no component near the product/commodity band — checked live, not assumed), so the opportunity is authored as a scenario on the same real edge and world models the fear scenario already uses, not pulled by the sweep. Exercised live in `twin/beat-intel.sh` steps 3a/3b (both scenarios run standalone and inside `twin sweep`, byte-identical, plural distinct forecasts each) and asserted in tests/test_intel_beat.py::test_the_opportunity_scenario_is_framed_as_a_win_not_a_threat, ::test_the_opportunity_scenario_is_built_on_the_real_euv_edge_not_a_toy_one (no `example.invalid` anywhere in the emitted artefact), ::test_the_opportunity_forecast_is_plural_and_the_ensemble_genuinely_disagrees, and ::test_no_outcome_is_authored_for_the_opportunity_scenario_either — the same permanent honesty as the fear scenario, since this org's story has not happened yet either way. The toy `euv-slip-2026` fixture in the walking-skeleton `intel` overlay (`fixtures.build()`, distinct from `build_intel_org`) is left as-is: it was never the real spine and this criterion was never asking for that fixture to change, only for the real spine to stop lacking an opportunity side.

PASS: a threat path and an opportunity path both ran end to end on the same dated state. The
shared-prior limitation travelled with the substrate result before either later claim was
made. Enactment was proposed and never disposed — versioned governance, spent before the
beat's concluding claim rather than after it. A non-technical lever and a technical control
were then priced in one unit, and which of them is cheapest depends on which causal account
you believe — reported as a curve with the disagreement first, never as a verdict, and the
last thing this beat computes. No score: this subject carries no answer key, and says so.

